<!--
     Device-owned copy of the stock vendor/etc/media_codecs_mediatek_audio.xml.

     SIX decoder declarations are removed relative to stock. Five of them had
     four libraries behind them, and those are no longer extracted:

       OMX.MTK.AUDIO.DECODER.GSM        libMtkOmxGsmDec.so
       OMX.MTK.AUDIO.DECODER.ALAC       libMtkOmxAlacDec.so
       OMX.MTK.AUDIO.DECODER.APE        libMtkOmxApeDec.so
       OMX.MTK.AUDIO.DECODER.ADPCM.MS   libMtkOmxAdpcmDec.so
       OMX.MTK.AUDIO.DECODER.ADPCM.DVI  libMtkOmxAdpcmDec.so

     The sixth is OMX.MTK.AUDIO.DECODER.WMA, and it is removed for a different
     reason: there is no component behind it at all. proprietary-files.txt
     extracts only libMtkOmxCore / libMtkOmxMp3Dec / libMtkOmxVdecEx /
     libMtkOmxVenc, and configs/mtk_omx_core.cfg has no WMA line, so by the
     rule stated further down this file OmxStore cross-checks the declaration
     against a live listNodes() and drops it, costing one INFO line per boot:
     "OMX.MTK.AUDIO.DECODER.WMA not found in IOmx". Declaring a codec the image
     cannot instantiate is not a capability, and it was also making the count
     above wrong.

     These are unmaintained vendor media parsers on a SoC that left MediaTek's
     security-bulletin coverage years ago, and they run inside mediacodec where
     any application or any media file can reach them. Published advisories name
     the GSM decoder (CVE-2022-20019, an incorrect bounds check requiring no
     privileges, with mt6755 in its CPE map) and the ALAC decoder (CVE-2021-0674
     / CVE-2021-0675, out-of-bounds read and write). Nothing is going to fix
     them.

     WHAT IS LOST: hardware decode of Apple Lossless, Monkey's Audio and the two
     ADPCM variants; there is no AOSP replacement for those three formats. GSM
     is NOT lost ; com.android.media.swcodec already ships
     c2.android.gsm.decoder. Everything mainstream (MP3, AAC, FLAC, Vorbis,
     Opus, WAV/PCM) is unaffected.

     TO REVERT: restore the five <MediaCodec> lines here, restore the five
     OMX.MTK.AUDIO.DECODER lines in configs/mtk_omx_core.cfg, and re-add the four
     libMtkOmx*Dec.so lines to proprietary-files.txt. The declarations and the
     libraries must move together: OmxStore keeps only XML entries whose
     component the OMX core actually provides, so a declaration without its
     library is silently dropped and a library without its declaration is dead.

     libMtkOmxVdecEx.so deliberately STAYS. mtk_omx_core.cfg routes HEVC, AVC,
     VP9, MPEG2/4, H.263, VC1, DIVX and XVID through it: it is the hardware
     video decode path, and there is no replacement. Accepted, knowingly.
-->
